[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"App Router vs Pages Router Architecture in Next.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next.js provides two distinct ways to build applications: the legacy Pages Router and the modern App Router. Understanding the architectural differences between them is crucial for modern web development, performance optimization, and scalable application design."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Pages Router Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Pages Router is file-system based and relies heavily on the 'pages' directory. Every file inside this directory automatically becomes a route. It relies on special functions like getStaticProps and getServerSideProps for data fetching."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// pages/index.js
export default function Page({ data }) {
  return <div>{data.title}</div>;
}

export async function getServerSideProps() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
  const data = await res.json();
  return { props: { data } };
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The App Router Architecture"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in Next.js 13, the App Router lives in the 'app' directory and is built on top of React Server Components (RSCs). It introduces a new file convention supporting layouts, loading states, error boundaries, and streaming out of the box."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// app/page.js
export default async function Page() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
  const data = await res.json();
  return <div>{data.title}</div>;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Architectural Differences"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default Rendering: Pages Router components are Client-Side Rendered by default unless data-fetching methods are used. App Router components are React Server Components by default."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Layouts: Pages Router uses a single _app.js for global layouts, making complex nested layouts difficult. App Router natively supports nested layouts via layout.js files."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Fetching: Pages Router uses getStaticProps and getServerSideProps. App Router uses standard async/await inside Server Components and native fetch extensions."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Routing and Special Files: App Router uses strict special files like loading.js, error.js, and not-found.js for granular UI states per route segment."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Migration Strategy"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"While the Pages Router is still fully supported, the App Router represents the future of Next.js with superior performance through streaming and reduced client-side JavaScript. You can incrementally migrate an existing application by running both routers side-by-side."}]
